{:edb/db-before-t 1431 :edb/db-after-t 1432 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534744 50 #inst "2026-10-08T05:39:12.353Z" 13194139534744 true] [17592186128835 1100 "For which points does the function $y=f(x)$ below have a removable discontinuity?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040337/assets/159566da49ef22ea1aae7800355949ae3cf02dac6a6a02e0a8bc344db176a75d.png)" 13194139534744 true] [17592186128835 1100 "For which points does the function $y=f(x)$ below have a removable discontinuity?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2002/Source/Images/8abe220313dcb5097dcb66f6b13d2190.png)" 13194139534744 false] [17592186128835 1145 "The function has a discontinuity at $x=-3$. Checking our continuity conditions, we have\n\n- $f(-3)= 2$ exists $\\,\\,{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n- $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to -3}f(x)= -2$ exists $\\,\\,{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n- $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to -3}f(x) \\neq f(3)$ $\\,\\,{\\color{red}{\\times}}$\n\nSo it's the third condition that failed, which tells us that $x=-3$ is a removable discontinuity. A similar argument applies for $x=3$, which is also a removable discontinuity. So, the function has removable discontinuities at $x=-3, 3$.\n\nNote that the function also has a discontinuity at $x=0$, but it is not a removable discontinuity. Checking the conditions, we find that\n\n- $f(0)= -2$ exists $\\,\\,{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n- $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 0}f(x)$ does not exist $\\,\\,{\\color{red}{\\times}}$\n\nSo this time, the second condition failed, and therefore $x=0$ is not a removable discontinuity." 13194139534744 false] [17592186128835 1145 "The function has a discontinuity at $x=-3.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have\n\n- $f(-3)= 2$ exists $\\,\\,{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to -3}f(x)= -2$ exists $\\,\\,{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to -3}f(x) \\neq f(3)$ $\\,\\,{\\color{red}{\\times}}$\n\nSo it's the third condition that failed, which tells us that $x=-3$ is a removable discontinuity. A similar argument applies for $x=3$, which is also a removable discontinuity. So, the function has removable discontinuities at $x=-3, 3.$ \n\nNote that the function also has a discontinuity at $x=0,$ but it is not a removable discontinuity. Checking the conditions, we find that\n\n- $f(0)= -2$ exists $\\,\\,{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 0}f(x)$ does not exist $\\,\\,{\\color{red}{\\times}}$\n\nSo this time, the second condition failed, and therefore $x=0$ is not a removable discontinuity." 13194139534744 true] [17592186128836 1100 "For which points does the function $y=f(x)$ below have a point discontinuity?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040337/assets/10ba0496a4dc42579bb95d371a65d808edc0cc5a5653ab3e23d4fe85adf90518.png)" 13194139534744 true] [17592186128836 1100 "For which points does the function $y=f(x)$ below have a point discontinuity?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2002/Source/Images/10672c90c91cf5d9de3a618d768abfd2.png)" 13194139534744 false] [17592186128836 1145 "The function has a discontinuity at $x=2$. Checking our continuity conditions, we have\n\n- $f(2)= 2$ exists $\\,\\,{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n- $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 2}f(x)= 1$ exists $\\,\\,{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n- $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 2}f(x) \\neq f(2)$ $\\,\\,{\\color{red}{\\times}}$\n\nSo it's the third condition that failed, which tells us that $x=2$ is a point discontinuity." 13194139534744 false] [17592186128836 1145 "The function has a discontinuity at $x=2.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have\n\n- $f(2)= 2$ exists $\\,\\,{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 2}f(x)= 1$ exists $\\,\\,{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 2}f(x) \\neq f(2)$ $\\,\\,{\\color{red}{\\times}}$\n\nSo it's the third condition that failed, which tells us that $x=2$ is a point discontinuity." 13194139534744 true] [17592186128837 1100 "Given the function $f(x)$, defined by\n${x + 3,\\begin{vmatrix}x < 0 \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}x = 0; 3 - x, \\mid x > 0$,\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $\\lim\\limits_{x\\to 0^+} f(x)=\\lim\\limits_{x\\to 0^-} f(x)$\n2. $\\lim\\limits_{x\\to 0} f(x)=f(0)$\n3. $f(x)$ has a removable discontinuity at $x=0$" 13194139534744 false] [17592186128837 1100 "Given the function $f(x),$ defined by\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x+3, & x<0 \\\\ 0, & x=0 \\\\ 3-x, & x>0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x\\to 0^+} f(x)=\\lim\\limits_{x\\to 0^-} f(x)$\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x\\to 0} f(x)=f(0)$\n\n- $f(x)$ has a removable discontinuity at $x=0$" 13194139534744 true] [17592186128837 1145 "Let's check our continuity conditions:\n\n- $f(0)=0$ exists $\\,\\,{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n\n- The left and right-sided limits are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}(x+3)=3, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}(3-x)=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the left and right-sided limits are equal, we conclude that statement I is true and\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\lim\\limits_{x\\to 0} f(x) = 3. \\,\\,{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}\n$$\n\n- The value of the limit is $\\displaystyle\\lim\\limits_{x\\to 0} f(x) = 3,$ while the value of the function is $f(0)=0.$ So, statement II is false and\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\lim\\limits_{x\\to 0} f(x)\\neq f(0)\\,\\,{\\color{red}{\\times}}\n$$\n\nSince the third condition failed, the function has a removable discontinuity at $x=0,$ and statement III is true.\n\nIn conclusion, only statements I and III are true." 13194139534744 true] [17592186128837 1145 "Let's check our continuity conditions:\n\n- $f(0)=0$ exists $\\,\\,{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n- The left and right-sided limits are\n$lim_(x → 0^{-})f(x) = lim_(x → 0^{-})(x + 3) = 3,; lim_(x → 0^{+})f(x) = lim_(x → 0^{+})(3 - x) = 3$.\nSince the left and right-sided limits are equal, we conclude that statement I is true and\n$\\displaystyle\\lim\\limits_{x\\to 0} f(x) = 3. \\,\\,{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n- The value of the limit is $\\displaystyle\\lim\\limits_{x\\to 0} f(x) = 3$, while the value of the function is $f(0)=0$. So, statement II is false and\n$\\displaystyle \\lim\\limits_{x\\to 0} f(x)\\neq f(0)\\,\\,{\\color{red}{\\times}}$\n\nSince the third condition failed, the function has a removable discontinuity at $x=0$, and statement III is true.\n\nIn conclusion, only statements I and III are true." 13194139534744 false] [17592186129019 1020 #edb/ref 17592186307085 13194139534744 true] [17592186129019 1020 #edb/ref 17592186307092 13194139534744 true] [17592186129019 1020 #edb/ref 17592186307099 13194139534744 true] [17592186129019 1020 #edb/ref 17592186307148 13194139534744 true] [17592186129019 1020 #edb/ref 17592186307155 13194139534744 true] [17592186129020 1020 #edb/ref 17592186307078 13194139534744 true] [17592186129020 1020 #edb/ref 17592186307120 13194139534744 true] [17592186129020 1020 #edb/ref 17592186307127 13194139534744 true] [17592186129020 1020 #edb/ref 17592186307134 13194139534744 true] [17592186129021 1020 #edb/ref 17592186307106 13194139534744 true] [17592186129021 1020 #edb/ref 17592186307113 13194139534744 true] [17592186129021 1020 #edb/ref 17592186307141 13194139534744 true] [17592186129021 1020 #edb/ref 17592186307162 13194139534744 true] [17592186129733 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534744 true] [17592186129733 1100 "Which of the following functions has a removable discontinuity at $x = 3$?" 13194139534744 false] [17592186129733 1100 "Which of the following functions has a removable discontinuity at $x=3?$" 13194139534744 true] [17592186129733 1145 "The function $q(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=3.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have\n\n- $q(3)=0\\,$  exists  $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 3}q(x)=1$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 3}q(x)\\ne q(3)$  $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nSo it's the third condition that failed, which tells us that $x=3$ is a removable discontinuity.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040337/assets/d7fc2426919c72ff934d19e98eb7465b8a42852407809d242213da9c26a4ed4a.png)\n\n   \n\nNotice that the functions $h(x)$ and $p(x)$ are continuous at $x=3,$ while $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ both have a discontinuity at $x=3$ but they are *not* removable." 13194139534744 true] [17592186129773 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534744 true] [17592186129773 1100 "Given that\n$g(x)|= {5x - 5,\\begin{vmatrix}x < 1 \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}x = 1; x^{3} - 1, \\mid x > 1$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $lim_(x → 1^{+})g(x) = lim_(x → 1^{-})g(x)$\n2. $lim_(x → 1)g(x) = g(1)$\n3. $g(x)$ has a removable discontinuity at $x = 1$" 13194139534744 false] [17592186129773 1100 "Given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(x) & ={\\begin{aligned}5x-5, & x<1 \\\\ 0, & x=1 \\\\ {x}^{3}-1, & x>1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}g(x)={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}g(x)$\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 1}g(x)=g(1)$\n- $g(x)$ has a removable discontinuity at $x=1$" 13194139534744 true] [17592186129773 1145 "Let's check our continuity conditions:\n\n- $g(1)=0$ exists $\\,✓$\n\n- The left and right-sided limits are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}(5x-5)=5(1)-5=0, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}({x}^{3}-1)={1}^{3}-1=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the left and right-sided limits are equal, we conclude that statement I is true and\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 1}g(x)=0.\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\n- The value of the limit is ${lim}_{x\\to 1}g(x)=0,$ and the value of the function is $g(1)=0.$ So, statement II is true and\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 1}g(x)=g(1)\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\nSo, the function is continuous at $x=0,$ and statement III is false.\n\nIn conclusion, only statements I and II are true." 13194139534744 true] [17592186307078 1017 #edb/ref 17592186307079 13194139534744 true] [17592186307078 1089 "q-104387" 13194139534744 true] [17592186307078 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534744 true] [17592186307078 1100 "Which of the following functions has a removable discontinuity at $x=-1?$" 13194139534744 true] [17592186307078 1113 #uuid "069bab20-c8c9-55e2-ad80-81ddc7c330e6" 13194139534744 true] [17592186307078 1145 "The function $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=1.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have:\n\n- $f(-1)=-3$ exists $\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to (-1)}f(x)=1$ exists $\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to (-1)}f(x)\\ne f(-1)$ $\\,\\times $\n\nSo it's the third condition that failed, which tells us that $x=-1$ is a removable discontinuity.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040337/assets/340443e20bc285d2e711b81608a60eb81ff33537ece23860b11871f5373dfde2.png)\n\nNotice that the functions $p(x)$ and $q(x)$ are continuous at $x=-1,$ while $g(x)$ and $h(x)$ both have a discontinuity at $x=-1$ but they are *not* removable." 13194139534744 true] [17592186307079 1000 #edb/ref 17592186307080 13194139534744 true] [17592186307079 1000 #edb/ref 17592186307081 13194139534744 true] [17592186307079 1000 #edb/ref 17592186307082 13194139534744 true] [17592186307079 1000 #edb/ref 17592186307083 13194139534744 true] [17592186307079 1000 #edb/ref 17592186307084 13194139534744 true] [17592186307079 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534744 true] [17592186307079 1041 "selection" 13194139534744 true] [17592186307079 1104 #edb/ref 17592186307083 13194139534744 true] [17592186307079 1110 #uuid "b281959e-3f6b-58ea-a79f-4478c4c7b384" 13194139534744 true] [17592186307080 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040337/assets/b77c43e9e33a958c65d6b3478b50c2f43e56d4a514bd092b50a9856d353a78f5.png" 13194139534744 true] [17592186307080 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139534744 true] [17592186307080 1111 #uuid "e032a4a5-0609-5f20-bed6-7913dde2af26" 13194139534744 true] [17592186307081 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040337/assets/e82c2ab5ec147cbf2f6a6370aa10caaf23effdd672a2496add4bd72d2e5a9ab9.png" 13194139534744 true] [17592186307081 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139534744 true] [17592186307081 1111 #uuid "02859795-38ef-5fda-8de2-4bedcb02ceb5" 13194139534744 true] [17592186307082 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040337/assets/c6c9826ddd0cfc64cfa288b365c20c2e8bd67398f8f049a3ff80db13ab979d3f.png" 13194139534744 true] [17592186307082 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139534744 true] [17592186307082 1111 #uuid "d5dfd831-51dd-5ac7-93bf-26ec100dc28e" 13194139534744 true] [17592186307083 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040337/assets/340443e20bc285d2e711b81608a60eb81ff33537ece23860b11871f5373dfde2.png" 13194139534744 true] [17592186307083 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139534744 true] [17592186307083 1111 #uuid "3d5bbf9c-4a90-5cd4-bb16-dc91c6793378" 13194139534744 true] [17592186307084 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040337/assets/f1b1db6867e4629ba3155f5de1d59cdf36ed9190dbd7a18733bcbcf2f10402c5.png" 13194139534744 true] [17592186307084 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139534744 true] [17592186307084 1111 #uuid "bdd7f6f2-bd12-5f09-9020-2c0fa3970135" 13194139534744 true] [17592186307085 1017 #edb/ref 17592186307086 13194139534744 true] [17592186307085 1089 "q-104407" 13194139534744 true] [17592186307085 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534744 true] [17592186307085 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040337/assets/039c88a5cb4278b5522ccb95cde8a92f48ae572deb3ad38845c2ceaaec1e90ac.png)\n\nConsider the function $y=f(x)$ plotted above. For what values of $x$ does the function $y=f(x)$ have a removable discontinuity?" 13194139534744 true] [17592186307085 1113 #uuid "0198f318-cd17-5473-a78f-6e641cab268c" 13194139534744 true] [17592186307085 1145 "The function has a discontinuity at $x=2.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have\n\n- $f(2)=-4$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 2}f(x)=2$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 2}f(x)\\ne f(2)$ $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nSo it's the third condition that failed, which tells us that $x=2$ is a removable discontinuity.\n\nNote that the function also has a discontinuity at $x=-2,$ but it is not a point removable discontinuity. Checking the conditions, we find that\n\n- $f(-2)=-2$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to (-2)}f(x)$ does not exist $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nSo this time, the second condition failed, and therefore $x=-2$ is not a point removable discontinuity.\n\nConsequently, the correct answer is $x=2$ only." 13194139534744 true] [17592186307086 1000 #edb/ref 17592186307087 13194139534744 true] [17592186307086 1000 #edb/ref 17592186307088 13194139534744 true] [17592186307086 1000 #edb/ref 17592186307089 13194139534744 true] [17592186307086 1000 #edb/ref 17592186307090 13194139534744 true] [17592186307086 1000 #edb/ref 17592186307091 13194139534744 true] [17592186307086 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534744 true] [17592186307086 1041 "selection" 13194139534744 true] [17592186307086 1104 #edb/ref 17592186307088 13194139534744 true] [17592186307086 1110 #uuid "53fee554-6da1-513b-a77a-e622c404beb5" 13194139534744 true] [17592186307087 1034 "$x=-1$ and $x=2$ only" 13194139534744 true] [17592186307087 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534744 true] [17592186307087 1111 #uuid "7546b801-cf8d-5593-bf2a-5850d9b0b105" 13194139534744 true] [17592186307088 1034 "$x=2$ only" 13194139534744 true] [17592186307088 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534744 true] [17592186307088 1111 #uuid "5477384e-c542-5ac2-b4a4-a2ea440e69ed" 13194139534744 true] [17592186307089 1034 "$x=\\pm 2$ only" 13194139534744 true] [17592186307089 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534744 true] [17592186307089 1111 #uuid "6243536a-3068-5adf-b9b1-df9d3894ca3d" 13194139534744 true] [17592186307090 1034 "$x=-2$ and $x=-1$ only" 13194139534744 true] [17592186307090 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534744 true] [17592186307090 1111 #uuid "3c9eddb6-84ba-5677-80e5-c048f19372d6" 13194139534744 true] [17592186307091 1034 "$x=-2$ only" 13194139534744 true] [17592186307091 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534744 true] [17592186307091 1111 #uuid "5548a840-8f1e-5294-a9d0-0b9c14bff39d" 13194139534744 true] [17592186307092 1017 #edb/ref 17592186307093 13194139534744 true] [17592186307092 1089 "q-104415" 13194139534744 true] [17592186307092 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534744 true] [17592186307092 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040337/assets/8050047cf4381ef77d5569ac23b2adaa6b8484f5be672fd9a52bf011beeb39e8.png)\n\nConsider the function $y=f(x)$ plotted above. For what values of $x$ does the function $y=f(x)$ have a removable discontinuity?" 13194139534744 true] [17592186307092 1113 #uuid "d9d00c8f-1806-5984-8025-b0f458ce1c2b" 13194139534744 true] [17592186307092 1145 "The function has a discontinuity at $x=-1.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have:\n\n- $f(-1)=2$ exists $\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to (-1)}f(x)=-3$ exists $\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to (-1)}f(x)\\ne f(-1)\\,\\times $\n\nSo it's the third condition that failed, which tells us that $x=-1$ is a removable discontinuity.\n\nThe function also has a discontinuity at $x=3.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have:\n\n- $f(3)=-2$ exists $\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 3}f(x)=1$ exists $\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 3}f(x)\\ne f(3)\\,\\times $\n\nSo it's the third condition that failed, which tells us that $x=3$ is also a removable discontinuity.\n\nFinally,  the function has a discontinuity at $x=5.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have:\n\n- $f(5)$ does not exists $\\,\\times $\n\nSo it's the first condition that failed, which tells us that $x=5$ is not a removable discontinuity.\n\nConsequently, the correct answer is $x=-1$ and $x=3$ only." 13194139534744 true] [17592186307093 1000 #edb/ref 17592186307094 13194139534744 true] [17592186307093 1000 #edb/ref 17592186307095 13194139534744 true] [17592186307093 1000 #edb/ref 17592186307096 13194139534744 true] [17592186307093 1000 #edb/ref 17592186307097 13194139534744 true] [17592186307093 1000 #edb/ref 17592186307098 13194139534744 true] [17592186307093 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534744 true] [17592186307093 1041 "selection" 13194139534744 true] [17592186307093 1104 #edb/ref 17592186307096 13194139534744 true] [17592186307093 1110 #uuid "99fc37e3-ebcc-5b27-ab70-c5470beaad28" 13194139534744 true] [17592186307094 1034 "$x=3$ only" 13194139534744 true] [17592186307094 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534744 true] [17592186307094 1111 #uuid "a7361aca-5afb-5143-a3bc-8dea88fa3a48" 13194139534744 true] [17592186307095 1034 "$x=-1$ and $x=5$ only" 13194139534744 true] [17592186307095 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534744 true] [17592186307095 1111 #uuid "515e4c47-e56a-5b01-bcf7-fa2403a9cd84" 13194139534744 true] [17592186307096 1034 "$x=-1$ and $x=3$ only" 13194139534744 true] [17592186307096 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534744 true] [17592186307096 1111 #uuid "604ae4f7-9372-505d-8442-f40455fceb5c" 13194139534744 true] [17592186307097 1034 "$x=-1$ only" 13194139534744 true] [17592186307097 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534744 true] [17592186307097 1111 #uuid "01e7db63-96fd-5041-a1cc-d7c10a7a2614" 13194139534744 true] [17592186307098 1034 "$x=3$ and $x=5$ only" 13194139534744 true] [17592186307098 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534744 true] [17592186307098 1111 #uuid "0a16e730-6882-588f-bea6-dd7edbfa4267" 13194139534744 true] [17592186307099 1017 #edb/ref 17592186307100 13194139534744 true] [17592186307099 1089 "q-104425" 13194139534744 true] [17592186307099 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534744 true] [17592186307099 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040337/assets/d869c61426504b7b24c2feebd60f68bca6f7dc8a2b40036bf5821e0208e1e197.png)\n\nConsider the function $y=f(x)$ plotted above. For what values of $x$ does the function $y=f(x)$ have a removable discontinuity?" 13194139534744 true] [17592186307099 1113 #uuid "12cf0cf4-20ea-574d-b3f6-b08a331df572" 13194139534744 true] [17592186307099 1145 "The function has a discontinuity at $x=2.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have:\n\n- $f(2)=3$ exists $\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 2}f(x)=0$ exists $\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 2}f(x)\\ne f(2)\\,\\times $\n\nSo it's the third condition that failed, which tells us that $x=2$ is a removable discontinuity.\n\nThe function also has a discontinuity at $x=5.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have:\n\n- $f(5)=-1$ exists $\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 5}f(x)=2$ exists $\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 5}f(x)\\ne f(5)\\,\\times $\n\nSo it's the third condition that failed, which tells us that $x=5$ is a removable discontinuity.\n\nFinally,  the function has a discontinuity at $x=-1.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have:\n\n- $f(-1)=3$ exists $\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to (-1)}f(x)$ does not exists $\\,\\times $\n\nSo it's the second condition that failed, which tells us that $x=-1$ is not a removable discontinuity.\n\nConsequently, the correct answer is $x=2$ and $x=5$ only." 13194139534744 true] [17592186307100 1000 #edb/ref 17592186307101 13194139534744 true] [17592186307100 1000 #edb/ref 17592186307102 13194139534744 true] [17592186307100 1000 #edb/ref 17592186307103 13194139534744 true] [17592186307100 1000 #edb/ref 17592186307104 13194139534744 true] [17592186307100 1000 #edb/ref 17592186307105 13194139534744 true] [17592186307100 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534744 true] [17592186307100 1041 "selection" 13194139534744 true] [17592186307100 1104 #edb/ref 17592186307105 13194139534744 true] [17592186307100 1110 #uuid "fe83238f-fc1a-5a5e-b094-66d4ff80d344" 13194139534744 true] [17592186307101 1034 "$x=-1$ and $x=2$ only" 13194139534744 true] [17592186307101 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534744 true] [17592186307101 1111 #uuid "30ae9b71-98ba-590d-a1cf-c4eb17c826eb" 13194139534744 true] [17592186307102 1034 "$x=2$ only" 13194139534744 true] [17592186307102 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534744 true] [17592186307102 1111 #uuid "a18d8c87-2cee-5776-9eea-acdc617efb74" 13194139534744 true] [17592186307103 1034 "$x=5$ only" 13194139534744 true] [17592186307103 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534744 true] [17592186307103 1111 #uuid "f01d7c76-903c-5419-98f7-dddc8172bb2d" 13194139534744 true] [17592186307104 1034 "$x=-1$ and $x=5$ only" 13194139534744 true] [17592186307104 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534744 true] [17592186307104 1111 #uuid "80b73b78-4fca-598d-823b-c9b8b4cd9573" 13194139534744 true] [17592186307105 1034 "$x=2$ and $x=5$ only" 13194139534744 true] [17592186307105 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534744 true] [17592186307105 1111 #uuid "8934eea5-143d-5d6d-a996-7a3ee55caabd" 13194139534744 true] [17592186307106 1017 #edb/ref 17592186307107 13194139534744 true] [17592186307106 1089 "q-104572" 13194139534744 true] [17592186307106 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534744 true] [17592186307106 1100 "Given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{x+11}, & x<5 \\\\ 12, & x=5 \\\\ {e}^{5/x}, & x>5\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements is true?\n\n- $f(5)$ exists\n- ${lim}_{x\\to {5}^{+}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {5}^{-}}f(x)$\n- $f(x)$ has a removable discontinuity at $x=5$" 13194139534744 true] [17592186307106 1113 #uuid "6d16c9ba-34ea-5f9d-9f33-6f6c8299fea0" 13194139534744 true] [17592186307106 1145 "Let's check our continuity conditions:\n\n- $f(5)=12$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, statement I is true.\n\n- The left and right-sided limits are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,{5}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,{5}^{-}}\\sqrt{x+11}=4, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\,{5}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,{5}^{+}}{e}^{5/x}=e\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that statement II is false and\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {5}^{+}}f(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to {5}^{-}}f(x).\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nSince the second condition failed, $x=5$ is not a removable discontinuity. So, statement III is false.\n\nIn conclusion, only statement I is true." 13194139534744 true] [17592186307107 1000 #edb/ref 17592186307108 13194139534744 true] [17592186307107 1000 #edb/ref 17592186307109 13194139534744 true] [17592186307107 1000 #edb/ref 17592186307110 13194139534744 true] [17592186307107 1000 #edb/ref 17592186307111 13194139534744 true] [17592186307107 1000 #edb/ref 17592186307112 13194139534744 true] [17592186307107 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534744 true] [17592186307107 1041 "selection" 13194139534744 true] [17592186307107 1104 #edb/ref 17592186307110 13194139534744 true] [17592186307107 1110 #uuid "bd41f9d3-e177-5f93-88a2-3ef6be301080" 13194139534744 true] [17592186307108 1034 "I, II, and III" 13194139534744 true] [17592186307108 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534744 true] [17592186307108 1111 #uuid "0468e042-4d2f-5f50-88f3-daa1e2261fb8" 13194139534744 true] [17592186307109 1034 "I and III only" 13194139534744 true] [17592186307109 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534744 true] [17592186307109 1111 #uuid "95688d2d-06a5-5284-ab99-5c0346344b8f" 13194139534744 true] [17592186307110 1034 "I only" 13194139534744 true] [17592186307110 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534744 true] [17592186307110 1111 #uuid "bc0f1e3f-fcd5-5ff6-b081-5cccc4a0ecb2" 13194139534744 true] [17592186307111 1034 "I and II only" 13194139534744 true] [17592186307111 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534744 true] [17592186307111 1111 #uuid "846e0a59-e5c5-5c8a-be6b-2edeaeeb50c7" 13194139534744 true] [17592186307112 1034 "II only" 13194139534744 true] [17592186307112 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534744 true] [17592186307112 1111 #uuid "e6c7c874-af2c-5ef6-be91-51c99748dd80" 13194139534744 true] [17592186307113 1017 #edb/ref 17592186307114 13194139534744 true] [17592186307113 1089 "q-104575" 13194139534744 true] [17592186307113 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534744 true] [17592186307113 1100 "Given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(x) & ={\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-34, & x<-3 \\\\ -25, & x=-3 \\\\ {x}^{3}+2, & x>-3\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to (-3)^{+}}g(x)={lim}_{x\\to (-3)^{-}}g(x)$\n- ${lim}_{x\\to (-3)}g(x)=g(-3)$\n- $g(x)$ has a removable discontinuity at $x=-3$" 13194139534744 true] [17592186307113 1113 #uuid "f9a3f2db-4157-50dc-996e-676dd4243a2e" 13194139534744 true] [17592186307113 1145 "Let's check our continuity conditions:\n\n- $g(-3)=-25$ exists $\\,✓$\n\n- The left and right-sided limits are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,(-3)^{-}}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,(-3)^{-}}({x}^{2}-34)=(-3)^{2}-34=-25, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\,(-3)^{+}}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,(-3)^{+}}({x}^{3}+2)=(-3)^{3}+2=-25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the left and right-sided limits are equal, we conclude that statement I is true and\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-3)}g(x)=-25.\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\n- The value of the limit is ${lim}_{x\\to (-3)}g(x)=-25,$ and the value of the function is $g(-3)=-25.$ So, statement II is true and\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-3)}g(x)=g(-3)\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\nSo, the function is continuous at $x=-3,$ and statement III is false.\n\nIn conclusion, only statements I and II are true." 13194139534744 true] [17592186307114 1000 #edb/ref 17592186307115 13194139534744 true] [17592186307114 1000 #edb/ref 17592186307116 13194139534744 true] [175921863071